  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - We address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by combining a genetic algorithm (GA) for global exploration with a gradient-based local refinement for exploitation.\n   - The GA uses a population of candidate solutions represented as step functions of fixed length (initially 180 samples, or random seed if not provided).\n   - The crossover uses a uniform selection of alleles between parents, while mutation introduces small random changes with probability control.\n   - The key improvement over simulated annealing is the deterministic global exploration via GA, balanced with efficient local searches.\n   - This approach tackles the high-dimensional (180-dimensional) optimization space by combining wide exploration with focused refinement, avoiding local optima that plagues simpler methods.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - **Representation**: Real-valued NumPy arrays of length n = 180 (if parent given) or any random seed if not. Each entry h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] and sum(h) = n/2 exactly after adjustment.\n   - **Objective**: Minimize C5 = max_{k} \u222b h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx over lag k, approximated by the discrete correlation maximum scaled appropriately.\n   - **Constraints**: Handle via projection onto box [0,1] and iterative sum adjustment when necessary.\n   - **Algorithm**:\n     1. Initialize population of 50 individuals using parent if available, or random generation.\n     2. Parallelize evaluation by computing C5 for batch.\n     3. Genetic operators: tournament selection, uniform crossover with 60% probability, stochastic mutation.\n     4. Local refinement: consecutive SLSQP calls with increasing beta, gradient-based, and warm restarts.\n     5. Maintain diversity via selection, early termination for steady trials, and adaptive mutation rates.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Validate input (seed, budget) and parent condition.\n   - Initialize population (50 samples of 180 variables) by either using parent (if provided) or random generation with sum adjustment.\n   - Define selection, crossover, mutation, and repair operators:\n     \u2022 Tournament selection: L=10 tournament size, K=50 selection count.\n     \u2022 Uniform crossover rate R=0.6, uniform mutation rate M=0.1 per gene.\n     \u2022 Mutation scale: 0.01 * (sign change direction) + keep sum via enforce_sum().\n   - Evaluate entire population in batches using np.correlate then scale (to approximate integral).\n   - Core loop:\n     - Elitism: keep 10% best and top 1 from previous.\n     - Tournament selection: pick parents for each new slot.\n     - Gene crossover: uniformly select between parents.\n     - Mutation: flip value with probability 0.1, then adjust sum.\n     - If time exceeds 20% of budget, switch to local refinement only mode.\n   - Local refinement:\n     \u2022 Apply SLSQP up to 3 times with betas (50,200,800,2000,8000,20000).\n     \u2022 Each has 300 function evaluations for gradient-based minimization.\n   - Warm restarts: every 50th generation, restart from the best solution found.\n   - Time allocation: GA uses 70% budget, SLSQP 30%.\n\n4. Search choices:\n   - Population size: 50 (high diversity).\n   - Fitness: directly C5 (lower is better).\n   - Mutation: scale \u22480.01 until bounds, then correct.\n   - Crossover probability: 0.6 to balance exploration/exploitation.\n   - Selection: tournament size 10 (average fitness picks on gradient local).\n   - Beta schedule: (50,200,800,2000,8000,20000) to probe global then local.\n   - Mutation rate: 10% per gene, applied conditionally when progress stalls (3 stagnant trials).\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Time estimation: GA phase ~70% = ~770s, function eval timing (\u22480.0004s) so ~35,000 evals.\n   - SLSQP: each 300 evals \u22480.12s, so 5 calls ~0.6s in budget.\n   - Check termination if n/a (batch operations), handle enforce_sum if stuck via boundary checks.\n   - Plan fallback: use the best parent if initialization fails, or random if parent not provided.\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Return numpy array (h) with sum dx = 1 and bounds [0,1] enforced.\n   - Recompute C5 before return from scratch.\n   - Add small sanity checks: ensure function evaluations have finite max, arrays are proper length.\n   - Final selection: best of GA-generated (with refinement) or fallback.",
